A||||3||owenslee||9.0:1 Hypereutectic 3.4L Pistons Available...!||||7||
Z||000000||owenslee||03-27-2001||03:30 AM||||On a lead from Keith @ Grooms engines I have been researching a source of Hypereutectic  replacement pistons for the 3.4L engine and located the following information:<P>Sealed Power Part #  H800P    9.0:1 C.R.<P>Hypereutectic FM244 Aluminum alloy w. 16.5% silicon<P>T-5 heat treatment.<P>Recessed dome .087" dp.  x 2.765" dia.<P>1.450" compression distance from wrist pin C/L to top of piston.<P>Top/second ring groove: 1.50mm wide<P>Oil ring groove: 4.00mm wide<P>Oversizes available: Std, .50mm, .75mm, 1.00mm<P>Price for reference: $36.22 ea. from Carparts.com<P>Ring set:<P>Sealed Power Premium Moly # E-548K<BR>Reference price (Carparts.com)  $178.13 set<P>Hope this info helps those rebuilding their 3.4L and looking for a better piston than OEM cast, without going to the expense of forged, or using 3.4 TDC 9.7:1 pistons that require premium gas.<P>Kerry<P>------------------<BR>"Badges?...Badges..??? We don gat to sho yu no stinkin' BADGES..!!!"||||reg||7
Z||000001||mrfixit58||03-27-2001||10:02 AM||||Thanks Kerry,<P>I've added it to my growing list of "3.4" parts and stuff.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||DandRauto||03-27-2001||12:01 PM||||If I wanter the higher 9.7 ratio what pistons do I use?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||owenslee||03-27-2001||11:26 PM||||DandRauto...<P>The 3.4 TDC piston giving 9.7 compression is  Federal Mogul # H684CP.<P>Keep in mind that you will need to run premium fuel with this compression.<P>The beauty of the 9.0 :1 piston is that you can run 87 octane regular with minimal loss in power at the lower compression. <P>Actually on a 3.4 with .5mm overbore you will pick up almost .2 point of compression so the difference in compression is minimal yet the difference in fuel cost could be substantial over the life of the engine.<P>For those that don't care about cost...go for it...just realize there is a penalty to pay for an increase in power that might be measurable at the track, but is unlikely to be felt on the street.<P>...Just my .02 from someone who built turbocharged, three stage nitrous, 4 cyl motorcycle engines for a living  and raced FunnyBikes 16+ years ago. <BR>(386 HP from 81 cu.in. using 28# boost and H&H Blue Racing Gas)<P>Kerry<P>------------------<BR>"Badges?...Badges..??? We don gat to sho yu no stinkin' BADGES..!!!"||||reg||6
